^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{5}^{2}-49\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(z-5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2}-49\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(z-5{)}^{2}-74.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-5$ and $q=-74.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511769 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "08c6d8d6-3d86-5074-bf99-0cb744c9412a" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression with a Negative Linear Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a9065e2-37e9-598b-a708-7db1109a4c02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-3,\\text{ }q=-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511735} {:answer/id #uuid "6c18d946-6e69-50be-87ed-92fd19d56764" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-3,\\text{ }q=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511740} {:answer/id #uuid "db54cc2a-edfc-550f-8892-f5aa690b9b72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=3,\\text{ }q=-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511758} {:answer/id #uuid "eff54709-4354-528a-a040-8a400ddfcf46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-6,\\text{ }q=-40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511763} {:answer/id #uuid "f3aa1d31-a517-54f2-8aab-3cd7c0f31ddc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-3,\\text{ }q=-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a9065e2-37e9-598b-a708-7db1109a4c02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-3,\\text{ }q=-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511735} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bab717f-01cb-58ed-b0d5-b19f53406b98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511727}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ff0f8a51-4ab6-507a-8e84-b31ad82bcba0" :question/problem "If the expression ${z}^{2}-6z-4$ is written as $(z+p{)}^{2}+q$, then what are the values of $p$ and $q?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}-2\\cdot 3z-4\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${3}^{2},$ writing the constant term $-4$ last:\n\n$$\n{{z}^{2}-2\\cdot 3z+{3}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{3}^{2}-4\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(z-3{)}^{2}-{3}^{2}-4\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(z-3{)}^{2}-13.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-3$ and $q=-13.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression with a Negative Linear Term"}]}
